<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daulat Khan Lodi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1526">1526</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daulat Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> was the governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>. Fearing the sultan, he invited the Timurid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the early 1520s, but then turned against him. He submitted to Babur in 1525 and died shortly afterwards, before Babur's victory at Panipat ended the Lodi dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daulat-khan-lodi.jpg" alt="Daulat Khan Lodi" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daulat_Khan_Lodhi_Searching_for_Baba_Nanak_in_the_River_Bein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Daulat Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> was the son of Tatar Khan, the previous Nizam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, who had asserted his independence from the Lodi dynasty under Bahlul Khan Lodi. Daulat Khan was first governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bist-doab">Jalandhar Doab</a> and was promoted to the governorship of Lahore between 1500 and 1504, serving under Sikandar Khan Lodi and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>. He was loyal to the dynasty but turned against Ibrahim, whose nature was rigid, proud and suspicious.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> tradition he is remembered as an employer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>. One of his officials, Jai Ram, was married to Nanak's sister Nanaki and found him work as keeper of Daulat Khan's store and granaries. When complaints were made that Nanak was wasting the stocks, two audits found them full and correctly accounted for, and when a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">Qazi</a> called Nanak to court over his teachings, Daulat Khan defended him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1523 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> was locked in a power struggle with his relatives and ministers, and Daulat Khan was one of his chief opponents, together with the sultan's uncle Alam Khan, then living under the protection of Sultan Muzaffar II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. Daulat Khan sent his son Ghazi Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, who returned with the warning that Ibrahim meant to remove him. He then sent messengers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, offering allegiance in return for help.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>'s army captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and Dipalpur. Daulat Khan and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> and Dilawar Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> joined him at Dipalpur, but Babur gave Daulat Jalandhar and Sultanpur instead of Lahore. Daulat and Ghazi went into hiding, while Dilawar accepted Sultanpur and the title of Khan Khanan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, leaving forces to help Alam Khan besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, Daulat Khan offered his help in the hope of recovering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, but was refused and left in charge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>. The attack on Delhi failed. Hearing that Babur was returning, Daulat and Ghazi fled to the fortress of Milwat, north of Lahore. Babur besieged it, Daulat Khan surrendered, and he died on the way to Bhera, where he was to be imprisoned. The invasion he had begun ended at the Battle of Panipat in 1526, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> was killed and Babur went on to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daulat Khan Lodi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Daulat Khan Lodi</b>, his governor of the Punjab, and his uncle Alam Khan invited Babur to invade, and so the sultanate was destroyed by its own internal divisions as much as by external force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
